            <ArticleTitle>Phlegmasia Cerulea Dolens in the Pediatric Population: A Life-threatening Condition.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e111-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jemermed.2015.05.037</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0736-4679(15)00600-9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Phlegmasia cerulea dolens is a rare complication of deep venous thromboembolism that can have life-threatening consequences. It is more commonly reported in the adult population, and rarely in the pediatric population, especially in a healthy child.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE REPORT" NlmCategory="METHODS">We report a case of a healthy 7-year-old girl who presented with right leg pain and fever, found to have a thrombus involving her inferior vena cava and right lower extremity. WHY SHOULD AN EMERGENCY PHYSICIAN BE AWARE OF THIS?: This case illustrates the importance of early recognition and a multimodal approach to treatment of phlegmasia cerulea dolens.</AbstractText>
